From 397f629a9b7ef7d2ee3e15e13dbb4e21c4343076 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gbm: more than 8 bits for video on nvidia-drm, through a
 half-float buffer

Video of more than 8 bits (HDR10, HLG, 10-bit SDR) asks for an output
of its depth: the window surface is made again in a format of 10 bits
per component for the time of the video. nvidia-drm has planes of 10
bits, but the EGL of the NVIDIA driver (615.71.09) has no window config
of them, only of 8 bits and of 16-bit floating point (hardware test of
2026-10-03):

  no 10-bit output; output formats AR24:plane XR24:plane AR30:no-plane
  XR30:no-plane AB30:plane XB30:plane; EGL window configs:
  R16G16B16A16:AB4H R5G6B5A0:RG16 R8G8B8A0:XR24 R8G8B8A8:AR24

So HDR10 went out with 8 bits per component.

When no fixed-point format of the depth has both a plane and an EGL
window, the window surface is now made of half-float components
(ABGR16161616F): on nvidia-drm only, known by the NV_INPUT_COLORSPACE
property of its planes, and while that property is "None". Read in the
open kernel modules 615.71.09: with no input colour space NVKMS takes a
floating-point surface without an input table and scans it out, like a
fixed-point one, through identity matrices and the identity output
table (the one that turns the values of a 10-bit surface back into its
codes). A transfer function
and tone mapping only come with the HDR metadata of the plane, which
Kodi does not set: it sets that of the connector. What Kodi draws does
not change: the same code values between 0 and 1 (PQ, HLG or SDR), now
kept at the precision of the buffer and sent at the depth of the link.
Dolby Vision keeps its 8-bit transport; a Dolby Vision stream that
falls back to the ordinary output gets the half-float one, like any
other video of 10 bits.

It fails safe:
- before anything is shown through the new surface, the driver is asked
  whether it takes such a buffer on this output (an atomic test commit,
  in which nvidia-drm validates). If it does not, or the surface cannot
  be made, the 8-bit surface is put back and the video plays through it
  as it did before. What the driver refused before the surface existed
  is not held against it; what a commit that it dropped before had
  carried is queued again there, like after any dropped commit (below);
- that question has neither the colorimetry and HDR metadata of the
  video, which are queued after it, nor a mode that is set later (a
  change of rate or of size during the video, full screen entered
  later, the display waking up). So when the driver refuses a commit
  while the output is a half-float one, the flip of a frame or a change
  of mode, the 8-bit surface is put back in the same presentation pass,
  a modeset is allowed again, and what the refused commit carried is
  queued again. Kodi itself shows the buffer with the state of the
  commit before, or nothing, and does not try a refused mode again. The
  refusal is remembered until it is looked at, because a refused change
  of mode is followed by flips in the old mode that are accepted. Not
  counted: a commit that only found the driver busy (EBUSY), one made
  while the output is meant to be off (it shows nothing), and the one
  that also turns off the outputs Kodi does not use. The first and the
  last of those still lose what they carried, as every commit does that
  the driver does not take; while the output is a half-float one the
  colorimetry and the HDR metadata are queued again after them, and
  such a pass does not count as one in which the output was shown.
  None of the 18 Kodi logs of the hardware tests so far has a refused
  commit, so one
  that comes with this buffer is taken to be about it. Putting the
  8-bit surface back does not turn the output on: a modeset is allowed
  through CDRMUtils::AllowModeset(), which leaves that as it is, where
  SetActive(true) would light a display that is meant to be off;
- after a failure the half-float output is left for that video and
  tried again with the next one (the player says when one starts; a
  renderer is also made again within a video). After three videos in a
  row it is tried with every eighth video only: a refusal is not always
  about the buffer, so none is taken to be for good. When the display
  is seen to be connected again, or to have another EDID, it is tried
  at once. That is seen where a new video looks at the display again,
  which it does only for a display that has offered Dolby Vision since
  Kodi started; no other display is looked at again, as in Kodi, and
  with those it waits its videos out. A video that it showed without a
  failure clears the count;
- the setting videoscreen.hadesfloatoutput (expert level, on by
  default, label and help translated in Estuary, shown on nvidia-drm
  only) turns it off;
- not with desktop OpenGL (which Generic does not build): only the GLES
  video shaders clamp, and only the GLES window system looks for
  refused commits.

Nothing clamps what is drawn into a floating-point buffer, so the video
shaders clamp their result when the output is one: values outside
[0, 1] from the YCbCr conversion or the overshoot of a scaling kernel.
The two shaders of the DRM PRIME renderer are built before the output
of a video is known, so they clamp always, which changes nothing on a
fixed-point output. The 0/0 in the Reinhard tone mapping of a black
pixel, which a fixed-point buffer stores as 0, is not computed.

Reading the output back (screenshots, captures) takes a floating-point
buffer as GL_FLOAT, whatever depth was asked for: it cannot be read as
bytes. It is known by the kind of its components, not by the type the
driver prefers for reading.

Making the surface again does not leave framebuffers behind: read in
the source of the driver's EGL platform library (NVIDIA/egl-gbm at tag
1.1.3, the version number of the library in 615.71.09; the binary
itself was not compared) and in the libgbm of the image (Mesa 26.2.4),
a surface frees its buffers with gbm_bo_destroy(), which calls Kodi's
callback that removes the framebuffer.

CEGLContextUtils::ChooseConfig() left the last config it had looked at
as the chosen one when none had the visual asked for. It now puts back
the one chosen before, which the surface in use was made with.

Not verified on hardware, any of it: that the picture is the one of
the 8-bit output but for its banding; whether the driver needs a
modeset for the change of format (one is allowed for it); and what the
output stage does with a value between two levels of the link (read in
the driver: it rounds to the nearest, and dithers when the link has 10
bits or fewer; under one level either way).
